    "source_text": "Global Markets & Credit Research · 6 November 2026\n07:55 SGT cutoff · 6 Nov; Treasury observations dated 5 Nov\n\nThe completed report text was recovered from the chat record; its formatted response and embedded links did not load in the browser. This extract includes only findings checked against accessible Reuters reporting or primary documents.\n\n1. U.S. Treasury par curve · 5 November 2026\n\n2Y 4.46% · 5Y 4.59% · 10Y 4.84% · 30Y 5.25%. The 10Y fell 6bp and the 30Y fell 3bp versus 4 November. These are official constant-maturity par yields. They are not coupons, original issue yields or live trade quotes.\n\n2. New issuance · Apple\n\nReuters reports a seven-part U.S. dollar offering of about US$7.5bn, based on a term sheet it saw on 5 November. The issuer’s preliminary prospectus does not establish all final pricing fields. Final issue prices, yields to maturity and spreads are omitted; no deal settlement is asserted. The on-page comparison of prior issues retains its stated date range.\n\n3. Credit watch\n\nThe Bank of England raised Bank Rate to 4.00% as inflation pressure persisted. Lower U.S. Treasury yields and tighter or wider corporate spreads are separate observations; a change in benchmark yields alone does not establish that issuer credit has improved. No ICE credit-index numbers are republished in this extract."
